  "body": "The meet law of the Rotary Flow kernel checks adjacent guide pairs only. The call site is `crates/motion/src/multiaxis/rotary_flow.rs`, in the band loop (`meet_or_cross`). A crossing between NON-adjacent guides is not checked. The kernel accepts such a set and machines it.\n\n## Reproduction\n\nThis reproduction comes from the safety-tier review of PR #4018 (2026-11-25). Build an R8 prism with 72 sides, from Z0 to Z32. Let `d = TAU / 72`. Give the guide chains in cylindrical coordinates `(theta, Z)`:\n\n- Guide 1: `(0, 0)` to `(0, 4)`.\n- Guide 2: `(3*d, 10)` to `(3*d, 14)`.\n- Guide 3: `(-d, 30)` to `(0, 2)` to `(d, 30)`.\n\nGuide 3 crosses guide 1 at `(8, 0, 2)` in world coordinates. The kernel accepts the set on main and on the PR #4018 head. The bands that the two adjacent pairs develop cross through the same ground, and no refusal states it.\n\n## What is wrong\n\nA set whose guides cross non-adjacently cannot tile. The kernel blends only the adjacent pairs, so the per-pair law never reads the crossing. The user gets passes that re-machine crossed ground, with no diagnostic.\n\n## What to do\n\nCheck every pair of picked guides for a meet or a crossing, not only the adjacent pairs. Refuse by name, at the crossing's own spot. The exact segment-pair law (Ericson 5.1) already exists in `rotary_flow_set::meet_or_cross`; extend the call to the complete set.\n\nThe workaround today: pick chains that do not cross.\n\nThis defect passes on main before PR #4018. The review of PR #4018 reported it as non-blocking and pre-existing. Issue #2829 already requests crossing-set refusals for this kernel.\n\nSuggested labels: P3, area:multiaxis, effort:high, source:agent\n",
